An entry point for Cooperative Linux, partway into head_64.S.

Everything above common_startup_64 exists to get from a bootloader's world into
the kernel's: relocation fixups, building early_top_pgt, and two CR3 writes to
install it and then init_top_pgt. A cooperative guest is already in the kernel's
world when it arrives -- the host built its address space, mapped the image at
its link addresses, and switched CR3 to it on purpose. Running that code throws
all of it away and installs page tables describing an identity-mapped physical
machine that never existed; the instruction after the CR3 write would be fetched
from an address space that does not contain it.

From this label down there is no CR3 write, so the remainder runs unmodified.

This mirrors what the i386 port has done since 2004, where ENTRY(co_arch_start_kernel)
sits at checkCPUtype in head_32.S for the same reason.

--- a/arch/x86/kernel/head_64.S
+++ b/arch/x86/kernel/head_64.S
@@ -195,6 +195,33 @@
 	 */
 	movq	%rax, %cr3
 
+/*
+ * Cooperative Linux enters here, and not at startup_64.
+ *
+ * Everything above this point exists to get from a bootloader's world into the
+ * kernel's: __startup_64 does the relocation fixups, builds early_top_pgt, and
+ * two `movq %rax, %cr3` install it and then init_top_pgt. A cooperative guest
+ * is already in the kernel's world when it arrives -- the host built its
+ * address space, mapped the image at its link addresses, and switched CR3 to it
+ * deliberately. Running that code would throw all of it away and install page
+ * tables describing an identity-mapped physical machine that was never there,
+ * and the instruction after the CR3 write would be fetched from an address
+ * space that does not contain it.
+ *
+ * From here down there is no CR3 write, so the rest runs unmodified: the CR4
+ * mask, the boot stack out of current_task, the kernel's own GDT, the data
+ * segments, EFER. The GDT load is not a problem -- the world switch saves the
+ * guest's GDTR on the way out and restores it on the way back in, so whichever
+ * table the guest adopts travels with it.
+ *
+ * initial_code is left alone here. The host points it at start_kernel before
+ * entry, because x86_64_start_kernel would call reset_early_page_tables() and
+ * assign init_top_pgt[511] -- CR3 hostility again -- and idt_setup_early_handler(),
+ * which would replace the fault stubs during the window they are most needed.
+ */
+SYM_INNER_LABEL(co_arch_start_kernel, SYM_L_GLOBAL)
+	ANNOTATE_NOENDBR
+
 SYM_INNER_LABEL(common_startup_64, SYM_L_LOCAL)
 	UNWIND_HINT_END_OF_STACK
 	ANNOTATE_NOENDBR
